docs/doc-O.md:4:A directed sequence of five rules suffices over the reals. It exposes a normalized weighted sum, replaces the full-row softmax with a summary of each prefix, partitions the prefix computation into tiles, and sinks the score producer into each tile. The experiment tests whether those steps also hold within a fixed f32 tolerance and let the CUDA backend match the performance of hand-written streaming attention.
docs/doc-O.md:83:The worktree already has a `differential()` invariant with independent baseline and subject runs and a domain-supplied comparator. A Node 24 probe calling it through `AnalysisManager.checkInvariant` accepted 1.125 and rejected 1.5 relative to 1 under an absolute budget of 0.25. `defineTransformation` and `applyPass` can gate each directed edit with the immediate input, origin and candidate as separate ports, as the Typed optimizer does for its original-program check. Use one pass per rule for the fixed sequence. A saturating search adds choices the experiment does not require. The missing Tix-specific piece at this interface is a rule-instance checker, plus the domain IR, evaluators and concrete invariant. [Differential invariant](../../../../../../src/framework/contracts/differential.ts), [pass API](../../../../../../src/framework/passes/define-pass.ts), [Typed optimization contract](../../../../../../src/typed/passes.ts).
docs/doc-O.md:85:Use 64 seeded random small cases with `N ≤ 129`, plus deterministic cases for `N = 1, B-1, B, B+1, 2B+1` for each candidate tile width `B ∈ {32, 64, 128}` and supported `D`. Include tied scores, a new maximum in each tile, an early dominant maximum, alternating-sign values, and saturated scores with tiny weights. Make partition and rebase checks fail on deliberate edits such as dropping a tail position and rescaling just `l`. Record rule version, input and output revisions, shape, seed or fixture, budget version, and first failing output index and values. Require a decision for every scheduled case. For generated CUDA, apply the same reference comparison to the returned device tensor. Parallel trees in tile reductions and CUDA elementary math can change rounding further. Keep score ordering and per-term division fixed, disable multiply-add contraction in the score loop, and use f32 throughout for the experiment.
docs/doc-O.md:97:## GB10 experiment
docs/doc-O.md:99:**Hypothesis.** From the plain Tix function, a fixed sequence of checked rule applications emits a single streaming f32 CUDA kernel that meets the fixed error budget and beats a materializing lowering, while approaching an independently hand-written streaming kernel. Treat a fused attention library call or a special-case handwritten replacement in the compiler as a failed derivation.
docs/doc-O.md:101:At `N ∈ {2048,4096}` with `D=64`, require all five rules to apply and pass their checks and require the CUDA outputs to pass on the device cases. Use identical seeded inputs across variants, check at least one seeded and one structured input per primary shape, and measure the following variants on one GB10 with fixed build settings:
docs/doc-O.md:107:The primary metric is median elapsed GPU time for the full device computation, with device DRAM read plus write bytes as a co-measurement of the memory claim. Warm up 20 times, time 100 runs with same-stream CUDA events, report median and interquartile range, and collect hardware-counter traffic in separate profiling runs. Keep inputs and outputs device-resident during timing, and report tile widths, launch geometry, toolchain and device. Freeze tile choices and arithmetic constraints before collecting the primary results. Check generated allocations and global stores for the absence of `N×N` buffers. Include `N=512` and `D=32,128` as secondary runs to see where launch overhead or state size changes the trade-off.
docs/doc-O.md:116:- Implement the tiled CUDA lowering, profiling harness and hand-written CUDA control when the backend is available.
docs/doc-D.md:49:| **R5. Sink producers, erase dead tensors** | Inline pure indexed definitions at their tile-local uses (or bind the exact expression once in the tile); erase a tensor only when it has no remaining uses or observations. Check the affine substitutions, bounds, def-use and type/extent preservation, and keep all roundings and reduction trees. In this application, one row owner holds all `x` components and advances blocks in order. | Each tensor element denotes its defining expression with the same indices; substitution is equality, and removing an unused pure definition cannot change the return. | Exact including f32: the same operations on the same inputs recompute the same bits, including signed zero, in the specified evaluator. Also run the original f64 gate for this new whole-program revision. This rule does not certify any new backend arithmetic or placement of register spills. |
docs/doc-D.md:74:Each row walks `j` once in tiles. A CUDA schedule can assign a query tile to one cooperative block, share its `k,v` loads among those queries, and keep scores/weights in tile-local storage. Parallelize across queries, scores and payload components without changing the stated reduction order in the initial experiment. Check block ownership, tail coverage, local lifetimes and index mapping when lowering; only the output is returned, with no global score or weight buffer. The point of the schedule is to test whether the derivation yields an efficient mapping, not to claim that removing a buffer proves a byte count.
docs/doc-D.md:81:- **Exact relation:** each rule instance has a local real equivalence certificate; R5 additionally preserves the f32 evaluation literally. Use an independent schema checker with versioned lemmas (finite-sum linearity, `exp(x+y)=exp(x)exp(y)`, the summary homomorphism and substitution) and registered validators for premises. The pass must not certify its own arbitrary rewrite. Test that incorrect rescale signs, mismatched reduction indices, a missing tail and erasing a live use are rejected by the checker, even on inputs that would mask the error.
docs/doc-D.md:82:- **Numerical relation:** for every output component `c` of a checked case, require equal shape, finite values and `|candidate_c − ref_c| ≤ 0.002 + 0.001|ref_c|`. The reference is the *original* Tix AST evaluated in f64 with the original grouping and lexicographic orders, without `fround` (not the previous f32 revision or a transformed f64 program). The absolute floor handles cancellation near zero; the constants are fixed proposed research thresholds for `|v|≤1`, not proven roundoff bounds. Check original f32 against the same gate as a control. Run it against every candidate IR revision and the actual CUDA outputs; CUDA exp/sqrt/division implementations may differ from the evaluator. Use f32 inputs/accumulators, no TF32, fast-math, intentional flush-to-zero, FMA contraction or reciprocal substitution in the initial comparison. Record compiler settings and inspect the generated code; don't infer CUDA bitwise equivalence. A numerical disagreement on a valid case is evidence against the candidate/contract, not an invitation to widen the thresholds after looking.
docs/doc-D.md:94:## GB10 experiment and decision rule
docs/doc-D.md:96:**Hypothesis.** Starting only with the given Tix AST, the ordered strategy R1→R2→R3→R4→R5 (match by structure) emits a single streaming kernel with no global `s/e` buffers, passes all prespecified local and numerical gates, and meets the following proposed thresholds on GB10. For each of the six primary shapes `N={1024,2048,4096}` × `D={64,128}`, the median GPU latency ratios satisfy **unfused/derived ≥ 1.2** and **derived/hand-streamed ≤ 1.25**. At `N=4096`, for both `D`s, measured off-chip read+write bytes are **at most 75% of unfused**. Report bytes for hand-streamed too; near parity or deviation helps diagnose the schedule. These are target outcomes, not predictions derived from big-O.
docs/doc-D.md:100:| Unfused | CUDA lowering of the original statements with explicit global score and exponential tensors; use competent tiled/coalesced kernels where applicable, not a one-thread reference evaluator. |
docs/doc-D.md:102:| Hand-streamed | An independent CUDA implementation of the running-max block algorithm, using the same f32 precision and math constraints, not a mixed-precision library substituted as a baseline. |
docs/doc-D.md:104:**Procedure.** Use the same resident dense buffers, layouts and workload, one GB10, and log device/driver/toolchain, flags, tile sizes, clocks/power regime and launch parameters. For both streaming variants, try the same fixed grid of `Bq∈{4,8,16}`, key block size `B∈{16,32,64}` on tuning inputs separate from test fixtures. Freeze each variant's fastest legal schedule per shape and gate those revisions before measurement. For timings warm up each variant (at least 20 evaluations); use CUDA event intervals for whole invocations (all unfused launches included), excluding allocation, compilation and transfers, in at least 100 interleaved repetitions per shape. Report medians, paired 95% bootstrap intervals on the ratios, and spread. Profile bytes in separate matched runs with the same cache starting protocol for all variants (cold-cache profiling via eviction outside the measured interval); sum validated device DRAM read/write counters across the invocation and name those counters. If GB10 does not expose usable counters, report bytes as unmeasured, not replaced by an allocation estimate. Also report temporary global allocation, spills, launch count, and signed/absolute error summaries. Timed fixtures must pass the same all-component reference check for all variants.
docs/doc-D.md:111:- Implement CUDA lowering and the GB10 benchmark fixtures, gate and profiling runs.
docs/doc-D.md:112:- Explore further CUDA schedules, tensor cores and mixed precision under separate contracts.
docs/doc-F.md:10:- **Reference and relation:** interpret the *original* Tix program on the same f32 inputs using f64 throughout, retaining source syntax and lexicographic reductions (including the original position of `/sqrt(D)`); no intermediate f32 rounding. Require the same shape and `abs(candidate[i,x] - ref[i,x]) ≤ 2e-4 + 2e-4*abs(ref[i,x])` everywhere, with both outputs finite. Freeze this proposed budget before the first run. Test the original f32 evaluator and the unfused CUDA baseline against it too: if the baseline fails, report that as failure of this experimental contract, not as a rewrite error or a reason to quietly widen the budget. Mixed absolute/relative error handles near-zero outputs from signed values; bitwise equality for all stages would disallow the intended regrouping. Sampled checks can refute, not prove, the f32 relation over this domain.
docs/doc-F.md:94:Use a directed sequence of versioned rules over a Tix adapter to the existing rewrite API. A fixed rule order suffices to test derivability; a general search would introduce a separate question about search cost and heuristics. Match on operators, index bindings and dataflow (including alpha-renamed inputs and statements); the guide to the schedule may name R1–R6 but must not supply a recognized attention kernel in place of a derived artifact. Before a rewrite publishes, use the host verification hook and a transformation invariant with `origin`, `before`, `candidate` ports. The exact local check reads `before` and `candidate` and associates the rule, binder/shape checks and proof schema with those revisions. Range facts for the numeric guard come from the declared domain and the matched arithmetic, not observed sample extrema. The differential invariant reads `origin` and `candidate`; its reference/cache identity includes the origin, domain, evaluator versions and corpus. Recheck the original relation at every step: a tolerance is not transitive, so a chain of adjacent checks would be unsound even as a sampled acceptance policy. Neither a `preserves` declaration nor the matcher alone counts as evidence. If a check rejects, keep that step and its counterexample in the trace, and do not count the derivation as complete.
docs/doc-F.md:96:The contract registry, rewrite acceptance hook and comparison callback in `differential.ts` are existing seams, not a Tix checker. The required domain components are the parallel Tix adapter/evaluators plus this small extension, rule validators and comparison wiring. Use complete decision coverage for the mandatory corpus: treat timeouts or evaluator exceptions on required cases as failed coverage rather than as agreement (the generic differential helper permits inconclusive cases). Record seed, shape, rule and artifact revisions, numerical policy, max error per case and first failing coordinate/input. Also check the actual GB10 outputs against the same reference at the lowering boundary; record contraction order, fusing multiply-add, exp implementation, denormal handling and any changed accumulation order. Use f32 throughout; no TF32 or f16 in the primary comparison. An independent CUDA comparison matters because validating a tuple interpreter does not validate code generation.
docs/doc-F.md:108:## GB10 experiment (not yet run)
docs/doc-F.md:110:**Hypothesis:** from plain attention Tix the directed schedule derives, without a hand edit, an f32 streaming CUDA kernel with an accepted trace at every rule and at lowering. For each `N ∈ {2048,4096,8192}` at `D ∈ {64,128}`, it uses at most half the device DRAM bytes and half the median GPU time of the unfused lowering, and takes at most `1.25×` the median GPU time of a hand-written streaming f32 kernel. These are proposed thresholds, not performance claims. A failure of any required match/acceptance, unexpected global score/weight temporary, or reproducible miss of any of those performance thresholds refutes the combined hypothesis. Report semantic and performance outcomes separately. Unavailable hardware or counters make the corresponding result unmeasured, not a pass.
docs/doc-F.md:114:- **Primary measurements:** CUDA-event median elapsed GPU time for the entire operator (all unfused launches included), and profiler DRAM read+write bytes per operator. Warm up with 20 calls, then collect 100 timed calls per variant and shape, interleave variants across rounds, and report median and IQR; repeat on a quiet, thermally stable GB10. Profile bytes in a separate run with documented cache/replay settings; also report global/L2 traffic if available to distinguish caching from eliminated requests. Fix and record device, driver/toolkit/profiler, power/clock conditions, flags and source revisions. Exclude allocation, transfers, compilation and profiler overhead from timing. Small/tail shapes are diagnostics, not primary performance gates.
docs/doc-F.md:115:- **Traffic model:** the compulsory input/output footprint is `Θ(ND)`, and the rewrite eliminates the `Θ(N²)` intermediate allocations and their materialization traffic. Total traffic is **not** generally `Θ(ND)` at a fixed on-chip tile size: with `B_r` query rows per CTA, logical K/V loads across CTAs can be `Θ(ceil(N/B_r) ND)` before cache reuse, and spilling can add more. Scores still take `Θ(N²D)` arithmetic. Measure rather than infer DRAM bytes from the footprint. If fusion reduces bytes but loses time, compare occupancy, registers/spills and instruction mix against the hand kernel before attributing the loss to the rule algebra.
docs/doc-F.md:120:- Implement the rule validators and CUDA lowering, then run the protocol on GB10.
docs/doc-H.md:8:The answer is designed, not yet built: a rule set of four derivation rules (plus one generic sibling and one variant), an extension of Tix with a `fold` construct (a reduction with a record-valued state, a pure per-element `step`, and a user-supplied `combine` whose **associativity over the reals is a checked certificate**), and a checking architecture that uses the framework's existing contract layer: every rewrite application is one transaction, its side conditions are decided syntactically at compile time, its real-arithmetic license is a lemma proved once per rule (not per application), and its f32 behaviour is gated after every step by a sampled tolerance check against an f64 reference interpreter of the same schedule. The derivation from the plain program to the streaming form is given as an explicit sequence, each intermediate program shown. Generality (softmax, log-sum-exp, softmax-then-contraction, layernorm statistics, multi-head, causal masking under the `-inf` extension) and the failure cases (index-domain mismatch, self-referential normalization, exact-f32 contracts, cross-row coupling) are stated with reasons. The experiment on the GB10 measures DRAM bytes and kernel time of the derived lowering against the unfused lowering and a hand-written streaming kernel, with falsifiable scaling and margin conditions, plus a mutation arm that measures the checking layer's own strength.
docs/doc-H.md:10:**Scope.** This ticket designs only: the rule set, the `fold` extension, the per-step checking scheme, the generality/failure analysis, and the experiment. It does not implement anything. The Tix front end, its evaluators, its f32 meaning and its meaning contract are parallel work and are treated as given interfaces; the CUDA lowering for Tix is assumed to exist with a tile-shaped allocation rule (see *Later*).
docs/doc-H.md:32:- **C2 (value, empirical).** The derived program, lowered to CUDA on the GB10, moves O(N·D) DRAM bytes (not O(N²)) and is competitive in time with a hand-written streaming kernel, while the checking layer is strong enough to reject deliberately broken variants of the streaming form.
docs/doc-H.md:276:**Where each check runs.** Each rule application is one framework rewrite transaction: `match` on the Tix statement graph, `guard` = the syntactic side conditions (registered pattern predicates — compile-time, no floats), `replace` = the fused form, `preserves` = the applicable meaning invariant. The host's semantic acceptance gate runs the invariant on the step's output revision and records, alongside the step's `TransformationResult`, the observed maximum relative deviation — so the derivation log is a per-step delta table, and each step's correctness argument is local: *this* match, *these* side conditions, *this* lemma, *this* measured delta.
docs/doc-H.md:307:**Hypothesis (falsifiable).** On the GB10, for `D = 64` and `N` swept over `{512, 1024, 2048, 4096, 8192}` (f32, no tensor cores in any arm):
docs/doc-H.md:313:**Arms.** K0 = unfused lowering of P0 (materializes `s`, `e` as global tensors); K1 = lowering of P4 (tile `T` autotuned over `{16, 32, 64, 128}`, best reported, choice recorded); K2 = a hand-written streaming kernel, same algorithm and tiling space, written directly in CUDA — the derivation must not lose to the thing people write by hand by more than the stated factor, or the derivation's *value* claim fails. All three arms are scalar-f32 SIMT so the comparison isolates the derivation, not ISA features.
docs/doc-H.md:315:**Measurement.** CUDA events around the function (inputs resident; H2D excluded), 50 warmup + 200 timed iterations, median and minimum of 3 repetitions; DRAM bytes from Nsight Compute's memory workload analysis (`dram__bytes.sum`) per launch, cross-checked against the analytic model; clocks locked where permitted, otherwise variance reported. The correctness arm re-runs the full case suite through `tix.meaning-within` on the *compiled* kernels' outputs where the harness can capture them, and on the f32 interpreter otherwise.
docs/doc-H.md:338:- R1–R6 as versioned rewrites with registered syntactic guards; priority order R1 → R2 → R3 → R4.
docs/doc-H.md:341:- Fold lowering to CUDA with tile-shaped allocation, autotuned `T` (depends on the parallel CUDA lowering work).
docs/doc-H.md:354:- `src/framework/contracts/differential.ts` — the sampled differential invariant mechanism this design's contracts are built on (cases, seeds, counterexamples, baseline/subject keys, worker decision).
docs/doc-H.md:355:- `src/framework/contracts/definitions.ts` — `defineInvariant`, `defineTransformation`, proof records and the assurance taxonomy the certificates ride on.
docs/doc-H.md:356:- `src/framework/ir/PATTERNS-AND-REWRITES.md` — the rewrite API and schedule semantics the rules apply through; per-step transactional evidence.
docs/doc-H.md:357:- `src/framework/ir/rewrites.ts`, `src/framework/ir/patterns.ts` — the matcher/guard/replace machinery and registered predicates the syntactic side conditions use.
docs/doc-H.md:358:- `src/typed/evaluate.ts` — the Typed domain's meaning contract (deterministic cases, `roots`, cached baseline/subject keys), the model `tix.meaning-*` follows.
docs/doc-H.md:359:- `src/typed/f32.ts` — the f32-on-bits op semantics that the Tix f32 meaning parallels (RNE per op, `Math.fround` of the double result).
docs/doc-M.md:4:**Proposal.** Derive a reduction on row summaries, not a call to an attention intrinsic. The useful fact about a block of scores is that changing the origin of *every* exponential multiplies *every* weighted sum by the same scalar. That fact determines the combine operation. Blocking then becomes an ordinary change of reduction tree. This is an exact derivation for real arithmetic; making the tree executable in f32 is a separate, testable claim. This ticket supplies neither a Tix implementation nor GB10 results.
docs/doc-M.md:41:`P` is a product of scalar additive folds. Require identical `j` domains and order, identical score and shift expressions, payload independent of the summary/shift, row and output-lane extents invariant in `j`. A tuple of sums equals the sum of tuples: projecting zero gives zero; projecting each componentwise add is exactly the old accumulator update. Pairing that result with the existing max changes no operations. This is also f32-exact with the specified left folds; `c_I` is computed *before* `P`. Apply at `I=J`, giving `finish(S(J))` with `finish(c,L,U)=U/L`. Packing alone still requires the row maximum before the weights.
docs/doc-M.md:50:follows by adding exponents and distributing a scalar over the same linear payload sums. Since summing over a concatenation adds the two sums, the only needed fields are the two existing `P`s and their origins. With `p=exp(c_A-h)`, `r=exp(c_B-h)`, emit
docs/doc-M.md:75:Thus each row visits a key block once, although its temporary scores are read twice on chip. Keep the row state across all blocks; drop the tile when consumed. Check the storage plan by def-use/lifetime: only `q,k,v,o` need global tensor storage, no `N×N` allocation, no global array of tile states. A fixed tile of `R` query rows with `B` keys needs scratch on the order of `RB+(R+B)D` floats including state and staged inputs; check actual registers, shared memory and spills, not just shapes. Arithmetic is still `Θ(N²D)`. **Linear footprint is not linear DRAM traffic.** Compulsory input/output bytes are `Θ(ND)`; without inter-query-tile cache reuse a query-major schedule issues `Θ(ceil(N/R)·ND)` K/V reads. The unfused stable softmax additionally writes and rereads quadratic intermediates. Avoiding those streams is the derivation's I/O claim; total GB10 off-chip bytes are a measurement, not an unconditional asymptotic promise.
docs/doc-M.md:79:Use the framework's distinct `requires` and `ensures` conditions (`src/framework/contracts/types.ts`). Use a directed matcher: walk backward from a returned contraction along SSA definitions, apply R1–R3 where their index patterns unify, try R4 only on a matching summary expansion, then R5. Permit reverse R4 only under a scheduled tile; this prevents an expand/fold cycle. Freeze the original program as the `origin` port; bind also the local before/after regions. An application receipt contains rule/version, substituted binders, extents, typed expressions, uses, certificate instance and, for R5, the exact tree and tile size. A small verifier reconstructs the RHS from the LHS and checks the side conditions above plus an unchanged surrounding context modulo renaming. For reverse R4 reconstruct the fold from the proposed expansion. The trusted real lemmas are substitution, distribution, coordinate projection, change of exponential origin and fold partition as proved above. A failed match supplies no license to rewrite. This is local proof by template instantiation, not empirical discovery of an identity.
docs/doc-M.md:81:Observation is **the entire returned tensor** (shape and elements), not the score matrix or an internal running state. At every step, including blocking, reverse expansion and final kernel, use `differential()` (`src/framework/contracts/differential.ts`) with seeded cases. Baseline: evaluate *original parsed source* on the same f32 inputs in binary64, with the original scopes, increasing index order and binary64 operations (no intermediate fround). Subject: current program under its recorded f32 semantics. Proposed fixed research relation: identical shape, both outputs finite and for every element `|subject-ref| ≤ 2e-4 + 2e-4*|ref|`. Freeze/version this domain and threshold with the Tix contract before the run. The absolute term matters for cancellations near zero. Run the unoptimized f32 origin through the same gate first; a baseline failure is a failed contract experiment, not evidence against a specific merge. No post-hoc tolerance widening. Sampled agreement with f64 is neither a proof for all f32 inputs nor bitwise preservation of the source.
docs/doc-M.md:102:## GB10 decision experiment (proposed, not run)
docs/doc-M.md:104:**Hypothesis.** Starting only with the given source and the rules above yields a kernel that passes all gates, avoids global quadratic temporaries, and at `(N,D)=(2048,64),(4096,64)` on one GB10 beats unfused time and DRAM traffic while approaching an independently coded block fold. The comparison is against hand-written **f32 streaming**, not a vendor low-precision tensor-core path.
docs/doc-M.md:106:1. Keep three artifacts: unfused materialization of all Tix statements (reasonable tiled contractions); rule-derived block fold with the full trace; independent hand-written streaming CUDA. Same row layout, precision/score scope, resident buffers, inputs and compiler/math policy. Match timing boundaries (all kernels from q,k,v ready to o ready); exclude allocations, compilation and host copies. Give both streaming variants the same candidate tile set, e.g. `R∈{8,16,32}`, `B∈{16,32,64}`, discard resource-infeasible choices, select by median time on separate tuning inputs, freeze and check the selected trees. Record unfused tuning and resource limits too.
docs/doc-M.md:107:2. Use primary shapes above, plus `N=1,63,65,256,1024`, `D=32,64,128` to diagnose crossover and tails. Record GPU, clocks/power policy, software versions, launch/scratch configuration, seed and build flags. Warm resident inputs with 10 runs; collect 50 synchronized CUDA-event total times per shape/variant in interleaved order. Report median and interquartile range. Separately profile the same workload with a consistent documented cache condition, summing measured device DRAM read **and** write bytes across launches (also report L2 traffic, spills and peak allocated temporaries to distinguish causes). Probe profiler support on the GB10 before running; allocation counts or inferred source bytes cannot substitute for DRAM counters.
docs/doc-M.md:113:- Lower and measure on GB10 when CUDA code generation and counters are available; no simulator performance proxy.
docs/doc-B.md:6:The derivation below proves equality over the reals. It also specifies which edits preserve the Tix f32 evaluation exactly and how to test the others. The research question for GB10 is whether the resulting checked schedule actually saves traffic and time. No GPU measurement is claimed here.
docs/doc-B.md:25:Use an **ordered fold with typed state**. Write `fold(j in I, empty, map(j), combine)` in the IR. The map and combine are pure expressions. State may contain a fixed tuple of f32 scalars and fixed-shape f32 vectors (here the existing shape name `D` sets vector length). A tagged empty state can stand beside a nonempty tuple. Construction, projection, the tag test and lexical `let` perform no arithmetic. A contiguous block is an index range with the same absolute indices as the source. These are internal forms, not new Tix source syntax.
docs/doc-B.md:90:Use the existing pre-publication `verify` hook. It receives the predecessor and a provisional result. Bind the **original parsed program** separately as the fixed baseline for the whole trace. The framework's `differential` invariant accepts side-specific evaluators and a comparison function and records seed, counts and counterexamples. A local probe on Node 24 passed all 38 existing tests in the two relevant modules, including host rejection and a differential invariant inside a transformation contract. This establishes the framework hooks. Tix supplies the domain-specific evaluator and relation as assumed in the brief. [Rewrite host](../../../../../../src/framework/ir/rewrites.ts) · [Differential checker](../../../../../../src/framework/contracts/differential.ts) · [Rewrite tests](../../../../../../tests/framework/ir-rewriting.test.mjs) · [Contract tests](../../../../../../tests/framework/ext-e5.test.mjs)
docs/doc-B.md:95:- **Every revision, including the source and the final GPU output.** Evaluate the original expression tree with the same f32 inputs widened to f64, double operations and functions, original parentheses and lexicographic reduction order, with no intermediate f32 rounding. Compare the candidate's returned f32 tensor to this fixed f64 result. Require identical shape, finite observations and, for every coordinate, `|candidate - ref| ≤ 2e-4 + 2e-4 * |ref|`. The absolute term matters near cancellation. These are proposed preregistered experiment settings, not a universal error bound or a claim about defaults in the parallel Tix work. Comparing neighbors alone could accumulate error because this relation is not transitive.
docs/doc-B.md:111:## Experiment on GB10
docs/doc-B.md:113:**Hypothesis.** For single-head attention on each primary shape `N ∈ {2048,4096}, D ∈ {64,128}`, the six schemas derive the schedule from the given source, every prescribed check passes, and a single derived CUDA kernel uses at most 75% of the unfused pipeline's device-memory bytes and elapsed device time, and at most 120% of the hand-written streaming kernel's time. The primary measurements are paired median device time and off-chip read-plus-write bytes for a complete forward call. Thresholds are proposed decision criteria.
docs/doc-B.md:115:Compare three artifacts on the same GB10 with the same resident row-major inputs, output, f32 precision and math settings. (1) Lower the original statements directly, materializing `s` and `e` and retaining the source division placement. (2) Lower the accepted block fold. (3) Write the same block schedule independently in CUDA, including ordered score arithmetic, tail handling and final division. Use the same tile sizes for (2) and (3), initially 16 query rows by 32 keys, and comparable row/column tiling where applicable in (1). Grouping independent rows inside a tile shares loads without changing the per-row fold. Use strict scalar f32 for all three, disable contraction, fast math and flush-to-zero, and check actual device outputs against the f64 reference. This isolates derivation and fusion; mixed-precision tensor-core code would confound the comparison.
docs/doc-B.md:118:2. Preload and prefault all inputs, allocate scratch before timing, and synchronize after warmup. Record device, compiler, driver, math flags, shapes, tile plan, and cache protocol. Use CUDA events on one stream spanning **all** kernels in a call, including the unfused intermediates. Exclude compilation, transfer and profiling. Use 10 warmup calls per variant, then 50 paired trials per shape, rotating the three variants' order across trials. Before each call evict prior trial data with a disjoint buffer sweep sized by the copy probe, outside the timed and counted region. Allow cache reuse within the call. Report per-shape median paired time ratios and their interquartile ranges.
docs/doc-B.md:119:3. In separate counter runs, repeat the same calls and aggregate device-memory-controller read and write bytes across all constituent kernels. Identify the available GB10 counter(s), units and memory level with a device-to-device copy probe first. Increase the eviction buffer until cold-copy read and write counts stabilize near the copy payload in each direction. Match this cache protocol across variants and across timing and counter runs. Report per-variant read and write totals, median paired byte ratios across 10 repeats, and temporary global allocation sizes. If off-chip counters cannot be validated, report the bytes hypothesis as unmeasured alongside the timing protocol.
docs/doc-B.md:129:- Once CUDA lowering and GB10 access are available, lower the accepted schedule and run the three-way comparison.
docs/doc-K.md:70:The max and sums may traverse tile scratch separately. Each query row advances through the key blocks once. A CUDA lowering can assign a tile of query rows to each thread block, reuse the current key/value tile across those rows, and keep their running summaries and score scratch on chip. It needs no global score or weight tensor. Working tile sizes, the live state and the storage plan belong to lowering. The expressions and dependencies come from the rewritten IR. A recognition pass that emits a hand-written attention kernel would be shorter, but it would not establish the individual rewrite claims.
docs/doc-K.md:98:## GB10 experiment
docs/doc-K.md:102:Measure `N ∈ {512, 2048, 4096}` and `D ∈ {64,128}` on the GB10, with `N = 4096` at both widths as the primary comparison. Start with a query tile of 16 rows and key blocks of 32. Give the derived and hand-written streaming variants the same tile dimensions, layout, input and output types, and f32 arithmetic policy. The unfused variant implements the source and materializes both square intermediates. Check the device output of all three against the f64 origin on one fixed-seed tensor triple per benchmark shape, plus the smaller correctness corpus. Preserve the per-term score division and the IR fold order in the CUDA lowering and disable arithmetic contraction and fast math for the initial comparison. CUDA implementations of transcendental functions and subnormal handling can still differ from the host. The device output gate tests those differences directly. This controlled baseline measures derivation and lowering overhead. A peak-speed vendor kernel could use a different precision or math policy and would not isolate that overhead.
docs/doc-K.md:112:- Add CUDA lowering for the explicit block schedule and run the GB10 experiment once the backend is available.
docs/doc-I.md:4:**Proposal.** Derive the online recurrence by changing the representation of a weighted sum: a partial sum carries the maximum at which its weights were computed. Merging two partial sums first puts them at a common scale. A CUDA lowerer sees a tiled fold, not an `attention` opcode. This is a research design; neither the rewrite checker nor the GB10 results exist in this worktree.
docs/doc-I.md:111:| R6 | Check exact capture-free substitution, local sharing, unchanged operation order, and no live uses of deleted definitions. | None in the IR evaluator. CUDA's functions, scheduling and arithmetic are checked separately. |
docs/doc-I.md:115:The current framework has transactional rewrites and versioned relational invariants, but no Tix verifier or f64 oracle in this worktree. Use a directed `applyOnce` sequence through the rewrite acceptance gate rather than general rule search; the experiment tests one derivation and needs an inspectable stopping point: require the structural/lemma check, then a domain-specific `differential()` invariant with `origin` as baseline (as in the existing Typed origin pattern), running f64 on `origin` and f32 on `candidate`. Supply the experiment's comparison and a pinned case manifest; the generic differential helper is not itself a tolerance policy. Treat any missing, inconclusive, nonfinite or out-of-tolerance required result as unqualified (even though the helper can report agreement among decided cases). Record origin/parent/candidate revisions, rule and evaluator versions, partition, seed/manifest, corpus coverage, worst ratios and first counterexamples. Reuse origin results only with the same origin and complete contract/corpus version. Accept a revision only after both gates; on rejection, don't claim the derivation reached a fused kernel. This needs a Tix adapter and a small instance verifier on the existing framework, not a change to the framework scheduler or a new prover.
docs/doc-I.md:119:Freeze a manifest before measuring: small and tail shapes (`N=1,2,31,32,33,63,64,65,257`, `D=1,7,64,128`), every benchmark shape below, and all candidate tile widths (also `B=1` in the small checks). On the small grid run 16 seeded draws per shape plus constructed cases; on every benchmark shape run two seeded draws and sharply separated / near-tied score cases. Use uniform bounded samples and include all-zero logits, repeated maxima crossing tile boundaries, successive maxima near the end of the row, extreme positive/negative scores (including f32 underflow of some weights), one-hot values, and signed/cancelling and near-zero outputs. Test mutation negatives (e.g. swap merge scales, drop the last key) so a verifier or harness accepting a deliberately wrong candidate fails the study. Use the same oracle for CUDA outputs on the corpus, including actual large shapes; budget that evaluation separately from kernel time. This is sampled numerical assurance, not a theorem for every f32 array in the domain.
docs/doc-I.md:129:| Layernorm raw moments | R2 and optionally R3 zip `sum_j x_j` and `sum_j (x_j*x_j)` in one pass for statistics; keep the existing mean and variance finalizers. Applying weights to the original row still reads it again unless it is retained. A two-pass centered variance does **not** match: converting it to `E[x²]-E[x]²` needs a new rule and may be numerically unacceptable near constant rows. |
docs/doc-I.md:133:## GB10 experiment
docs/doc-I.md:135:Run this study in two stages once the Tix frontend, evaluators and contract are available: qualify the revision trace in the evaluator first, then qualify and measure the CUDA artifacts. The staged design distinguishes a missing derivation from a slow lowering.
docs/doc-I.md:137:**Hypothesis.** The unannotated source (and variants with renamed tensors/indices) reaches an accepted single tiled CUDA kernel through R1–R6 using no attention-specific lowering substitution. On **each** primary shape `N in {4096,8192}`, `D in {64,128}`, it moves at most half the off-chip bytes of the materialized lowering, takes at most `1/1.5` of the materialized lowering's median time, and takes at most `1.25×` the median time of the hand-written streaming comparator. Both the accepted revision chain and the GPU outputs must pass the frozen numerical qualification.
docs/doc-I.md:139:**Lowering under test.** One thread block owns a tile of `Bq` query rows; hold its running `(m,l,u)` in registers/shared storage, load each key/value tile of `Bj` rows, compute its score tile, summarize, merge, discard, and write the query output tile once. The backend should lower tile-local producers, product state, and a loop from generic IR constructs; it must not infer the recurrence from a library call or function name. Use a fixed small search grid `Bq in {8,16,32}`, `Bj in {32,64}`; exclude configurations that do not fit target resource limits and apply the same tuning budget to both streaming variants on separate tuning data. Keep `d` in source order with separate rounded multiplies, divides and adds; no mixed precision, fast math, FMA contraction or flush-to-zero for the experiment. Within-tile `j` trees, if used, are explicit schedule changes with an exact real-law check for the affected fold and a fresh origin comparison. Hardware `exp`/`sqrt`/division may differ from the JS-rounded reference even then. Apply the tolerance gate to the actual GPU artifact and not just a simulation. All baselines use the same input types and arithmetic policy.
docs/doc-I.md:141:**Comparators and metrics.** Compare the derived kernel with (1) a competitive materialized lowering of the same source that writes global `s` and `e` and (2) a manually scheduled CUDA kernel for the same online recurrence, with no compiler rewrite rules. Time the entire attention call's GPU kernels, with data device-resident and allocations out of the timed region. Use CUDA events after warm-up (e.g. 20 calls), report median and p10/p90 for 100 calls with interleaved variants on the same GB10. Separately profile hardware off-chip read plus write bytes for all kernels of each call, starting each profiled call with a cold GPU cache. Do not flush between kernels of the same call; log the cache control used. This separates bytes moved by the call from whether its inputs happened to survive a previous timed repetition. Report ratios against both baselines, launch count, peak global scratch and register spills; report `N=1024,2048,4097` as secondary shapes to inspect launch overhead, scaling and tails. Record target, CUDA/tool and driver versions, clocks/power mode, arithmetic modes and tile choices. Missing or unreliable byte counters leave the byte claim untested, not established by timing or by allocation size.
docs/doc-I.md:150:- Build and measure the experimental CUDA artifacts when the target and profiling tools are available.
