docs/doc-E.md:10:The tree specifies every combine, its left/right order and the position of the identity: default is a left fold from identity in increasing index order; an empty fold returns identity. A nested fold can map consecutive ranges to summaries. Over the reals, a registered identity/associativity lemma permits changing the tree on reachable states. A user's assertion of associativity does not suffice. In f32 **the tree is semantics**: evaluate leaf expressions, selected case bodies and each scalar coordinate with the core's per-operation rounding, including `exp`, with no implicit contraction. Projection and packing do not round again. Never infer f32 associativity from the lemma. Parallel evaluation of independent coordinates is free; changing their internal accumulation order is not.
docs/doc-E.md:40:The resulting schedule, for each resident group of query rows, is: load Q; initialize one empty accumulator per row; visit each K,V block once for this group; compute the row/block scores in the original `d` order; compute local `m`, then local `(l,p)` using scratch, merge and discard scratch. Finally write `p/l` once. Singleton blocks give `Present(a_j,1,u_j)` over the reals. The carried `p` is *unnormalized*: merging two already divided outputs by these coefficients alone is wrong. No global score or weight store remains if there are no other live consumers (require this for the storage claim). Local maxima and sums can scan on-chip scratch twice without scanning the global key stream twice. Schedule must fit query tile, K/V tile, accumulator and score scratch in registers/shared memory; spilling is a measurable cost, not part of the proof.
docs/doc-E.md:46:Pre-register as a trial relation: equal shapes and finite results with `|candidate-reference| <= 2e-4 + 2e-4*|reference|` independently at every coordinate. Absolute error matters for cancellation. Reference is the **unchanged source** on exactly the same f32 inputs widened to binary64, evaluated in its written order with f64 operations; it is not a mathematical oracle for all real inputs. First qualify the original f32 evaluator against it. For R1 and R3 require exact adjacent f32 agreement (including zero sign) in addition to the structural proof; for R2, R4, R5 require the local real certificate and the sampled tolerance gate. Every intermediate, including exact rewrites, and the final GPU kernel must pass against the same original f64 output. Tolerance does not compose; comparing adjacent candidates alone could silently spend the budget several times. A strictly exact-f32 contract does not authorize this pipeline.
docs/doc-E.md:48:Use `src/framework/contracts/differential.ts` as the existing baseline/subject invariant interface: plug in the parallel Tix evaluators and tensor comparator when available. Record origin/candidate revisions, rule witness, tree, seed, case index, worst absolute error and first failing coordinate, expected/actual and replay inputs. Require every scheduled case decided; do not count missing GPU runs as successes. Sample fixed seeds 21–25 on uniform bounded inputs, and directed fixtures: `N,D=1`, lengths just below/at/above block size, non-power-of-two D, all scores tied, increasing/decreasing and late block maximum, repeated maxima across a boundary, ±4 scores' input factors producing underflowed weights, and alternating ±1 payloads with nearly zero answer. Include largest shapes. Check the split locally on empty, singleton and unequal-length summaries too. Negative matcher tests: divisor depends on `j`, changed score between max and exp, overlapping tiles. Disable fast math, FMA contraction, TF32 and flush-to-zero for the trial; CUDA transcendental results may still differ from JS, hence test the emitted kernel. Replay a failure with the named schedule; reject rather than loosen tolerance after seeing results. Passing samples is evidence, not a uniform f32 error bound.
docs/doc-E.md:57:## GB10 decision experiment (not run)
docs/doc-E.md:59:Compile the literal source three ways: unfused SSA materialization; the witnessed rewrite chain with tiled schedule; independent hand-written blocked f32 CUDA computing the same function. All three must pass the contract. Use identical resident row-major inputs, math settings and output layout on one GB10; record driver/toolkit, clocks, actual tile sizes, register/shared usage and spills. Give both streaming variants the same tile search budget (query rows 8/16/32, keys 16/32/64, feasible configurations only); select on a separate seed and freeze before measuring. No compilation, allocation or host transfer in the timed region; do include every unfused kernel launch. Time with GPU events, 10 warmups then 50 synchronized trials per shape/variant, interleaved order. Report median and interquartile range. In separate profiler runs collect device DRAM read+write bytes summed across kernels, with identical cache policy (evict before each measured trial using a buffer larger than last-level cache, outside timing). Report peak global scratch from allocation plan as a separate number.
docs/doc-E.md:61:Sweep `N=256,1024,2048,4096`, `D=32,64,128`; include a tail case `N=1025`. Primary endpoint fixed in advance: at `(4096,64)` derived median time <=80% of unfused and <=125% of hand-written, and derived DRAM bytes <=75% of unfused. Require no global `N×N` allocation in derived code. Any unmet threshold, failed checked rewrite, or in-domain counterexample refutes the corresponding claim for this design. Separate failure of matching, numerical acceptance, spilling, and compute-bound timing in reporting. A missing GB10 measurement leaves the performance hypothesis unanswered.
