docs/doc-M.md:2:# From row softmax to a checked block fold
docs/doc-M.md:4:**Proposal.** Derive a reduction on row summaries, not a call to an attention intrinsic. The useful fact about a block of scores is that changing the origin of *every* exponential multiplies *every* weighted sum by the same scalar. That fact determines the combine operation. Blocking then becomes an ordinary change of reduction tree. This is an exact derivation for real arithmetic; making the tree executable in f32 is a separate, testable claim. This ticket supplies neither a Tix implementation nor GB10 results.
docs/doc-M.md:6:## Contract and notation
docs/doc-M.md:18:## One IR addition: a fold with product state
docs/doc-M.md:24:## Rules and their application, in order
docs/doc-M.md:28:**R1 — Substitute a producer at its reads.** `t[p]=E(p); …t[f(j)]…` becomes `…E(f(j))…`, with capture-avoiding substitution of the full index tuple, bounds checked, the same typed operations and reduction binders in the same order. Drop a definition only when dead. Conversely a pure common expression at equal indices may be let-bound at the same value. Lookup of an immutable definition denotes its RHS; deterministic rounding at identical nodes makes this f32-exact as well. Apply to expose `a_j`, `c=max(j) a_j`, `b_j=exp(a_j-c)` and `L=sum(j) b_j`; these row-local names denote shared expressions, not a commitment to tensor storage. For this elimination require no escaping score/weight use. Result: `o[x]=Σ_j ((b_j*w_j[x])/L)`.
docs/doc-M.md:30:**R2 — Take an invariant denominator out of a linear sum.** `Σ_j ((b_j*w_j[x])/L) → (Σ_j b_j*w_j[x])/L`. Also recognize `(b_j/L)*w_j[x]` by the same scalar identity. Require `j` not free in `L` and `L ≠ 0`: here `N>0` and the maximum contributes `exp(0)=1`. Distribution of the constant `1/L` through addition proves equality componentwise. The rewrite eliminates per-term divisions but rounds an unnormalized sum before the final divide; not f32-exact. We now need exactly `c,L,U[x]=Σ_j b_j*w_j[x]` and finish with `U/L`.
docs/doc-M.md:32:**R3 — Pack co-indexed statistics.** Abstract `c,L,U` as the projections of a summary `S(J)`. This is definitional notation with an expansion, not a primitive with an assumed merge law. For any nonempty interval `I` and any fixed origin `t` define
docs/doc-M.md:43:**R4 — Change origin and lift to a fold.** Match `S(I)` by the R3 expansion. Replace it by a left fold of `Present(a_j,1,w_j[:])` with `Empty` and the following combine. To derive, concatenate adjacent disjoint index intervals `A,B`. For two nonempty intervals the maximum is `h=max(c_A,c_B)`. In each interval `T` the identity
docs/doc-M.md:59:**R5 — Partition a certified fold and summarize the leaves by block.** For positive tile width `B`, replace `fold(J)` by the left fold of the folds on `[bB,min((b+1)B,N))` in increasing `b`. Check the cover (including last partial tile), no overlap/reordering, identical captures, and an identity/associativity certificate for the carrier (here R4). By unit and associativity, inserting units at boundaries and reparenthesizing the concatenated leaves preserves the real result. Use **R4 in reverse** inside each block to compute `S(tile)` by its R3 expansion rather than a serial merge per score. This is another checked application: the reverse direction still needs the finite-domain and tolerance checks. Blocking changes f32 grouping; the reverse step trades repeated rescaling for tile-local maxima and direct tile-local exponentials. Keeping a singleton fold would avoid block scratch but serialize more exponentials on the critical path.
